<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucknow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucknow is the capital of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the river Gomti. It became the capital of the Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> in 1775 and flourished as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> poetry, music, architecture and cuisine. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> its British Residency was besieged for months, and the city remains known for its Nawabi monuments and courtly culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucknow.jpg" alt="Lucknow" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harzratganj_Market,_Lucknow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lucknow stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomti River</a>, which meanders through the city and divides it into the Trans-Gomti and Cis-Gomti regions. It lies in the middle of the Indus-Gangetic Plain, surrounded by rural towns and villages, among them the orchard town of Malihabad and Kakori.</p><p>According to one legend the city is named after Lakshmana, a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, who is said to have had a palace or estate there called Lakshmanapuri. The settlement was known as Lakhanpur or Lachhmanpur by the 11th century, and later as Lucknow, the anglicised form of the local Lakhnau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 6th to the 4th century BCE Lucknow was part of Kosala, one of the 16 Mahajanapadas of the late Vedic period. From 1350 onwards it and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> region were ruled in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the Sharqi Sultanate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, the Nawabs of Awadh, the British East India Company and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. The Nawabs of Lucknow took that name after the reign of the third Nawab, when Lucknow became their capital.</p><p>In 1856 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> moved its troops to the border and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, alleging maladministration, and placed it under a chief commissioner. Lucknow was one of the major centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. It also took an active part in the independence movement. At the Congress session of 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> met for the first time, and the Lucknow Pact was signed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a>, the last Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, was a patron of Kathak, a classical dance form that is said to have originated in Lucknow. Under royal patronage the bawarchis and rakabdars, the chefs and royal cooks, developed the Nawabi cuisine of Awadh, with its slow dum cooking. The city's culture comes from the blending of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim rulers, and it is known for the ghararas of the Nawabs of Awadh and for chikankari embroidery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lucknow is the capital of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> and the administrative headquarters of its district and division. It had a population of 2.8 million at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011. It has been the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s Central Command since 1 May 1963, and it has a bench of the Allahabad High Court. Its monuments of the Nawabi era include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-imambara">Bara Imambara</a>, known for its Bhul Bhulaiya, the Chota Imambara and the Rumi Darwaza. UNESCO has named it a City of Gastronomy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucknow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He at once subdued the autonomous Shaikhzadas of <b>Lucknow</b> and Raja Mohan Singh of Tiloi, consolidating Oudh as a state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The All-India Muslim League, founded at Dhaka in 1906 to protect the interests of Muslims as a minority, won separate electorates in 1909 and agreed a joint programme with the Congress in the <b>Lucknow</b> Pact of 1916.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Early printed editions appeared at Lahore, Gujranwala, Amritsar and <b>Lucknow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Translations into Hindustani appeared at Lahore in 1870 and Gujranwala in 1879, and a Hindi translation at <b>Lucknow</b> in 1887.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li><li><blockquote>Nawabs such as Kalb Ali Khan and Hamid Ali Khan patronised Urdu poetry, Hindustani music and scholarship, making Rampur a refuge for artists from Delhi and <b>Lucknow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rampur-state">Rampur State</a></li></ul></section>
